---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { href, REPO_BRANCH, REPO_URL } from "../lib/site";

const licenseUrl = `${REPO_URL}/blob/${REPO_BRANCH}/LICENSE`;
---

<Base
	title="License"
	description="Pebble3D's licence in plain English: free, including commercial use, for organizations under USD $50,000 a year in revenue plus funding; a commercial licence above that; players never need one."
	nav="license"
>
	<section class="wrap page-head">
		<p class="eyebrow">License</p>
		<h1>Free for small teams. Players never need a license.</h1>
		<p class="lede">
			Pebble3D is source-available under the Pebble3D Engine License, © 2026 RJV Solutions.
			This page is a plain-English summary; the <a href={licenseUrl}>LICENSE file</a> is the
			binding text.
		</p>
	</section>

	<article class="wrap prose">
		<h2 style="margin-top: 0">The short version</h2>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>Free, including commercial use,</strong> if your annual revenue plus funding
				is under <strong>USD $50,000</strong>. You can use, copy, modify and run the engine,
				and ship it inside your games and apps.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>At or above USD $50,000</strong>, you need a separate commercial licence
				from RJV Solutions before you use it.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Players never need a license.</strong> End users of a game or app built
				with Pebble3D can play or use it freely.
			</li>
		</ul>

		<h2>What counts toward the $50,000</h2>
		<ul>
			<li>
				All gross revenue from any source, whether or not it comes from the game, plus all
				funding raised: equity, loans, grants and crowdfunding.
			</li>
			<li>Measured over the most recent trailing twelve months.</li>
			<li>
				Counted across your whole organisation and its affiliates (entities that control,
				are controlled by, or share control with you). Splitting a business across
				entities to stay under the threshold is not allowed.
			</li>
		</ul>

		<h2>If you cross the threshold</h2>
		<p>
			You have 60 days from the date you first reach $50,000 to get a commercial licence or
			stop using the engine. Products you distributed before that date can stay with their
			users.
		</p>

		<h2>Conditions</h2>
		<ul>
			<li>Keep the licence file and copyright notices with every copy you distribute.</li>
			<li>
				Anyone who gets the engine from you gets it under the same licence, and the revenue
				rule applies to them separately. You can't sublicense it under other terms.
			</li>
			<li>
				RJV Solutions may ask you in writing to confirm you are under the threshold; you
				must answer truthfully within 30 days.
			</li>
			<li>
				If you breach the licence and don't fix it within 30 days of written notice, your
				rights end.
			</li>
		</ul>

		<h2>Third-party parts keep their own licences</h2>
		<p>
			three.js (MIT), Rapier (Apache-2.0), the other npm and Cargo dependencies, and the
			demo assets downloaded by <code>pnpm fetch-assets</code> (CC BY 4.0, CC0 and MIT
			works) are not covered by the Pebble3D licence. See <a href={href("credits/")}>Credits</a>.
		</p>

		<h2>No warranty</h2>
		<p>The software is provided as is, without warranty, and without liability for damages.</p>

		<p class="callout">
			Need a commercial licence? The licence asks you to contact RJV Solutions. Read the
			full text: <a href={licenseUrl}>LICENSE on GitHub</a> or
			<a href={href("LICENSE")}>as plain text</a>.
		</p>
	</article>
</Base>
